model {
  extend basalt.toolsGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "Creating a pool commits its app, token, account link, record and one provisioning job in a single transaction, and answers from that commit alone: no call to the pool Worker can fail a pool that is already saved. The job is a member job named after the pool's app; it sends the pool's audience through the access hook, reads that audience when it runs so a later sharing change is included, and never runs member setup. The creating request runs the job once before it answers, through the queue's request runner and with the bound team installation uses, so a pool Worker that answers has the audience before the request does. A refused, unanswered or slow attempt leaves the job queued, and the provisioning queue retries it on its own schedule and attempt limit; an attempt that is killed waits for the queue's stale-claim scan. After the last attempt the job is failed like any other job and is not queued again. A repeat of the same creation request returns the saved pool, creates nothing, and runs the job again unless it was delivered; a pool saved before these jobs existed gets its job on that repeat. Recovery of a missing pool account link does not count the job, waiting or failed, as that owner's setup job. Before delivery the pool Worker admits only the owner named in the pool's own token; a new pool is private, so no one is waiting for a revocation. The access lifecycle is imported beside the provisioning job's own failure tracing; the poll keeps its trace."
    }
  }
}
